      en: "The Plaiul Fagului (Beech Land) Natural Reserve, located in central Moldova within the Ungheni and Călărași districts, was established in 1992 and spans 5,642 hectares. It represents a vital fragment of the Moldovan Codru forests and holds significant ecological value as it marks the easternmost limit of the European beech's (Fagus sylvatica) natural range. Historically, the reserve was formed from the former Rădenii Vechi hunting grounds to preserve the biodiversity of the hilly forest landscape. The geography of the area is defined by steep slopes and deep ravines, where beech trees grow alongside oak, ash, and hornbeam. Scientists have documented over 900 species of plants here, making it one of the most floristically diverse regions in the country. The dense woodlands provide a sanctuary for various mammals, including deer, wild boar, and the rare European wildcat. Today, the reserve serves as an important genetic pool for forest species and is an increasingly popular destination for ecological tourism.",
